{
  "image_name": "e7690eb49872c90480efc88f4e40d4e6.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares two manufacturing routes for battery electrodes and highlights where different defects can arise along each process. The top scheme shows a conventional wet route: slurry mixing, casting onto a current collector, drying, calendering, and winding. Along this line, the callouts indicate typical problems such as raised coating edges after casting, non-uniform particle migration during drying, crack or defect formation in the electrode layer, adhesion failure at interfaces, and finally delamination or fracture during winding.\n\nThe bottom scheme shows a dry route based on powder mixing, fibrillation, calendering, lamination, and winding. Here the visible issues are somewhat different: particle aggregation early in the dry mixture, cracking of the active material network, adhesion failure during lamination, wrinkling of the current collector, and again delamination or fracture when the finished electrode is wound.\n\nA small legend identifies the main components in both routes: active material, binder, conductive additive, and current collector. Overall, the image emphasizes that wet and dry electrode fabrication involve different processing steps and therefore different dominant failure mechanisms, even though both can end with similar mechanical damage in the final rolled electrode.",
